{
  "id": "3e973c15-561d-4266-a84c-63747b0e6e9c",
  "productId": "openmrs",
  "title": "EncounterService.saveEncounter REST Usage",
  "updatedAt": 1793548560374,
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "V6w4816C0mi2To3Q",
      "productId": "openmrs",
      "question": "If the behaviour of `EncounterService.saveEncounter` in openmrs-core changed, which code in the REST web services module (openmrs-module-webservices.rest) uses it and could be affected?",
      "generation": "g-b59a21",
      "kind": "grounded",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "In `openmrs-core`, `EncounterService.saveEncounter` is the API method that persists a patient encounter (a record of a clinical visit: when, where, who saw the patient, plus its obs, orders, diagnoses and so on) [1]. The implementation, `EncounterServiceImpl.saveEncounter`, does much more than write a row: it checks edit privileges, assigns a visit for a new encounter, rewrites obs datetimes/locations when the encounter date or location changed, re-points orders to the patient, saves obs, conditions, allergies and diagnoses, and finally saves the encounter itself [2].",
          "citations": [
            1,
            2
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Inside `openmrs-module-webservices.rest` (the module that exposes OpenMRS data over REST, with one \"resource\" class per domain object and per OpenMRS version), four production call sites reach this method.",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## Confirmed call sites in the REST module",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "| Where | What it does | Why it is affected |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `EncounterResource1_8.save` [3] | Returns `saveEncounter(enc)` | This is the base save for `POST`/`PUT /ws/rest/v1/encounter`. Any change to validation, privileges, visit assignment or cascaded saving changes the HTTP response and what a client sees on create/update. |\n| `EncounterResource1_9.save` [4] | Links each `EncounterProvider` back to the encounter, then calls `saveEncounter`, then returns the delegate | Same endpoint as above for OpenMRS 1.9+. The \"hack\" of setting the parent encounter exists because providers were otherwise persisted without `encounter_id`; if the service starts managing providers itself, this workaround becomes redundant or conflicting. It is also the save used by subclass `EncounterResource2_2` [5], which serves OpenMRS 2.2+ and adds `diagnoses` to creatable/updatable properties, so diagnosis handling in `saveEncounter` directly affects that endpoint. |\n| `EncounterProviderResource1_9.save` [6] | Calls `encounter.addProvider(...)`, then `saveEncounter`, then re-reads the encounter's providers to return the persisted one with its UUID | This is the `POST /encounter/{uuid}/encounterprovider` sub-resource. Because the new provider is only stored through the encounter's own save call, changes to how the service persists encounters/providers break UUID assignment and the method then throws `APIException(\"Encounter Provider not properly saved\")` [7]. |\n| `EncounterProviderResource1_9.delete` [8] | Marks the matching providers voided and calls `saveEncounter` on the parent encounter | `DELETE /encounter/{uuid}/encounterprovider/{uuid}` relies on the encounter save to flush the voided providers. Its comment ties it to TRUNK-5017; service-side changes to voiding or cascades would alter this behaviour. |",
          "citations": [
            3,
            4,
            5,
            6,
            7,
            8
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## Indirect users (not direct callers)",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "These resources in the same module save related data through other services, so they only feel a change if the encounter service's behaviour is reached transitively:",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "- `OrderResource1_8.save` [9] uses `OrderService.saveOrder`, not `saveEncounter`. However, `saveEncounter` itself now saves the encounter's orders and order groups [10], so an encounter created via `POST /encounter` with an `orders` property (allowed in `getCreatableProperties` [11]) is affected. This is a cross-resource dependency worth testing even though there is no direct `saveEncounter` call.\n- `DiagnosisResource2_2.save` [12] calls `DiagnosisService.save`. The link is the reverse direction: `saveEncounter` also persists the encounter's diagnoses [13], so `POST /encounter` with `diagnoses` (only offered by `EncounterResource2_2` [14]) goes through this code.",
          "citations": [
            9,
            10,
            11,
            12,
            13,
            14
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## Tests in the module that exercise the behaviour",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "These will fail or need updating if the semantics change: `EncounterController1_9Test` saves encounters directly [15] and again at line 553 [16], and `OrderController1_10Test` sets up an order by saving its encounter [17]. `EncounterResource1_8` is also instantiated directly in tests to check representation conversion [18].",
          "citations": [
            15,
            16,
            17,
            18
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "```archify\n{\"schema_version\":2,\"diagram_type\":\"workflow\",\"meta\":{\"title\":\"REST calls into saveEncounter\"},\"lanes\":[{\"id\":\"rest\",\"label\":\"webservices.rest\"},{\"id\":\"core\",\"label\":\"openmrs-core\"}],\"nodes\":[{\"id\":\"enc18\",\"lane\":\"rest\",\"col\":0,\"type\":\"backend\",\"label\":\"EncounterResource1_8.save\",\"sublabel\":\"POST/PUT encounter\"},{\"id\":\"enc19\",\"lane\":\"rest\",\"col\":1,\"type\":\"backend\",\"label\":\"EncounterResource1_9.save\",\"sublabel\":\"links providers\"},{\"id\":\"enc22\",\"lane\":\"rest\",\"col\":2,\"type\":\"backend\",\"label\":\"EncounterResource2_2\",\"sublabel\":\"adds diagnoses\"},{\"id\":\"prov\",\"lane\":\"rest\",\"col\":2,\"type\":\"backend\",\"label\":\"EncounterProviderResource1_9\",\"sublabel\":\"save and delete\"},{\"id\":\"svc\",\"lane\":\"core\",\"col\":3,\"type\":\"backend\",\"label\":\"EncounterServiceImpl.saveEncounter\",\"sublabel\":\"privileges, visit, cascades\"}],\"edges\":[{\"from\":\"enc18\",\"to\":\"enc19\",\"label\":\"subclass\",\"role\":\"branch\"},{\"from\":\"enc19\",\"to\":\"enc22\",\"label\":\"subclass\",\"role\":\"branch\"},{\"from\":\"enc19\",\"to\":\"svc\",\"label\":\"delegates\",\"role\":\"main\"},{\"from\":\"prov\",\"to\":\"svc\",\"label\":\"delegates\",\"role\":\"main\"}]}\n```",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## What I could not verify",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "I did not read the REST module's full controller/registry layer, so I cannot rule out other indirect paths such as AOP advice or a drop-in `EncounterService` subclass supplied by another module at runtime — those would be invisible to a static reference search. The emrapi module also calls `saveEncounter` in several places (e.g. `AdtServiceImpl` [19] and `EmrOrderServiceImpl` [20]), but that is a separate repository from the one you asked about and does not go through `openmrs-module-webservices.rest`; I mention it only in case it matters to your change.",
          "citations": [
            19,
            20
          ]
        }
      ],
